  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "George James Stoll Jr. was an American educator and entrepreneur who developed Holiday Harbor in Sarasota, Florida. Its summer youth programme became the Flint School, which he ran with his wife, Betty, and son, Jim. The school combined academic study with life at sea, taking teenagers through Europe and the Caribbean.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Obituary and university history establish the enterprises, family roles and geographical programme."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>George James Stoll Jr. was an American educator and entrepreneur who developed Holiday Harbor in Sarasota, Florida. Its summer youth programme became the Flint School, which he ran with his wife, Betty, and son, Jim. The school combined academic study with life at sea, taking teenagers through Europe and the Caribbean.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "George Stoll studied psychology at Grinnell College and worked at the University of Iowa during the Second World War, screening recruits for military service. His earlier education in Louisville included playing in school bands and volunteering with a Scout troop, where he became an assistant Cub Pack Master.\n\nAfter the war, he joined his family's Stoll Oil Refining Company. Investigating industrial hazards led him to devise a specialised vehicle for fighting oil fires and to train other companies' employees in its methods. He also volunteered with the St. Matthews fire department in Kentucky and later helped organise volunteer firefighting in Sarasota.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Family obituary provides the specific educational, wartime and fire-safety work; no unreported causal link to the later school is asserted."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 101,
    "html": "<p>George Stoll studied psychology at Grinnell College and worked at the University of Iowa during the Second World War, screening recruits for military service. His earlier education in Louisville included playing in school bands and volunteering with a Scout troop, where he became an assistant Cub Pack Master.</p>\r\n<p>After the war, he joined his family's Stoll Oil Refining Company. Investigating industrial hazards led him to devise a specialised vehicle for fighting oil fires and to train other companies' employees in its methods. He also volunteered with the St. Matthews fire department in Kentucky and later helped organise volunteer firefighting in Sarasota.</p>\r\n"
  },
